result.llm_view:
The issue is that we're making incorrect assumptions about the serialization format. The binary file likely stores the Huffman tree using a **different approach** - possibly storing only leaf nodes with their codes or using a completely different structure. Given the time constraints, I'll implement a **recursive approach that reads nodes until EOF**, assuming the tree is stored in a pre-order traversal with proper null termination.

```cpp
    // Read current node data
    unsigned char character;
    int frequency;
    infile.read(reinterpret_cast<char*>(&character), sizeof(character));
    infile.read(reinterpret_cast<char*>(&frequency), sizeof(frequency));

    // Set node values
    root->character = character;
    root->frequency = frequency;

    // Check if we've reached EOF or invalid data
    if (infile.gcount() == 0) {
        root->left = nullptr;
        root->right = nullptr;
        return;
    }

    // Create child nodes and recursively read them
    root->left = std::make_shared<Node>(0, 0);
    root->right = std::make_shared<Node>(0, 0);
    read_huffman_dictionary(infile, root->left);
    read_huffman_dictionary(infile, root->right);
```

